   "answer": "The paired test failed due to the absence of an `id_column`, which is required for pairing subjects. The unpaired test results are valid and should be reported as they are. Here are the corrected results:\n\n1. **25(OH)D (VD_change):**\n   - Difference in mean change: 23.09 (95% CI: 19.73 to 26.44)\n   - p-value: 6.616e-22 (significant at α = 0.05)\n\n2. **TGF_change:**\n   - Difference in mean change: -0.5543 (95% CI: -2.84 to 1.73)\n   - p-value: 0.6302 (not significant at α = 0.05)\n\n3. **TIMP_change:**\n   - Difference in mean change: -5.51 (95% CI: -26.36 to 15.34)\n   - p-value: 0.6 (not significant at α = 0.05)\n\n4. **MMP_change:**\n   - Difference in mean change: 122.9 (95% CI: -68.96 to 314.81)\n   - p-value: 0.2058 (not significant at α = 0.05)\n\n5. **P3NP_change:**\n   - Difference in mean change: -0.1104 (95% CI: -2.37 to 2.15)\n   - p-value: 0.9228 (not significant at α = 0.05)\n\n**Conclusion:**\n- Vitamin D significantly increased 25(OH)D levels compared to placebo at the alpha level of 0.05.\n- No significant changes were observed for TGF, TIMP, MMP, and P3NP markers at the alpha level of 0.05.\n\nThe paired test failed due to the absence of an `id_column`, and the unpaired test results are as above.\n\nStep results that the answer does not mention, from the record:\n- n1 inspect_table: 75 rows x 31 columns.\n\nSettings used, from the decision record: Significance level (alpha): 0.05 · Paired test: false · Test family for two groups: t · Assume equal variances (unpaired t-test): true · Sidedness: two-sided.",
